WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.040
So what if the reason you can remember your past, but you completely cannot remember your future has literally absolutely nothing to do with a ticking clock on the wall?

00:09.100 --> 00:09.400
Right.

00:09.640 --> 00:16.960
And it has everything to do with the fact that your brain is actively destroying alternate timelines just to keep you sane.

00:16.960 --> 00:21.500
It is a profoundly unsettling thought when you actually internalize it.

00:21.940 --> 00:25.820
Because we walk around assuming we are passengers on this train called time, right?

00:26.200 --> 00:28.380
Moving steadily through a landscape called space.

00:28.380 --> 00:35.240
Exactly. We treat space and time as these passive pre-existing containers, the ultimate foundational givens.

00:35.300 --> 00:37.620
Yeah. You just don't question the box you live inside.

00:37.780 --> 00:51.880
You don't. But today we are taking a deep dive into a brand new, incredibly dense and, I mean, frankly, paradigm shattering paper that was just published today, Monday, May 11th, 2026.

00:52.000 --> 00:53.420
Which is incredibly exciting.

00:53.420 --> 01:02.320
It really is. It's by an independent researcher named Flyxion, and the title is The Persistence of Structure, From Shape Space to Recursive Persistence.

01:02.380 --> 01:03.120
It's a bit of a mouthful.

01:03.400 --> 01:04.420
Yeah, a total mouthful.

01:04.820 --> 01:11.900
But our mission for this deep dive is to guide you through what Flyxion calls an ontological inversion.

01:12.340 --> 01:14.600
Which is exactly what it sounds like, a total flip.

01:14.600 --> 01:21.720
Right. We are going to unpack the argument that the universe is not governed by a pre-existing geometric background at all.

01:22.400 --> 01:29.220
Instead, reality might be entirely constructed by the local rules of what is admissible.

01:29.740 --> 01:33.120
Like what is actually allowed to survive from one moment to the next.

01:33.120 --> 01:37.080
And, you know, we are not just talking about abstract cosmology here.

01:37.200 --> 01:41.960
What makes this paper so compelling is that it builds a totally unified framework.

01:42.260 --> 01:46.100
It takes the deepest, most intractable problems in physics.

01:46.180 --> 01:47.420
Like the arrow of time and all that.

01:47.520 --> 01:54.920
Exactly. The arrow of time, the expansion of the universe, gravitational anomalies, and it connects them mathematically to the architecture of human cognition.

01:55.160 --> 01:55.800
Which is wild.

01:55.800 --> 02:04.120
It really is. It argues that the fundamental rules dictating whether a galaxy holds together are the exact same rules dictating how your memory functions.

02:04.420 --> 02:05.400
Okay, let's unpack this.

02:05.840 --> 02:15.180
Because to grasp how massive of a leap Flyxion is making, we kind of need to understand the corner that modern physics has painted itself into.

02:15.460 --> 02:17.940
Right. The historical context is crucial here.

02:18.040 --> 02:21.720
Yeah. And the audience listening right now, you know, you probably already know the basic narrative.

02:21.720 --> 02:28.700
We know Newtonian classical mechanics treated reality like a fixed stage with an absolute clock.

02:29.020 --> 02:31.680
The giant invisible metronome in the sky.

02:31.800 --> 02:38.100
Exactly. And we know Einstein came along, smashed the clock, warped the stage, and gave us dynamical space-time.

02:38.460 --> 02:43.620
But Flyxion argues that even with relativity, physics is still clinging to this massive crutch.

02:43.900 --> 02:49.920
Yes. The paper refers to the history of physics as a series of ontological compressions.

02:49.920 --> 02:52.380
Yes. Ontological compressions. Meaning what, exactly?

02:52.620 --> 03:00.100
Meaning that even though relativity made space-time flexible and interactive, it preserved a very specific, deeply ingrained assumption.

03:00.520 --> 03:06.100
The assumption that lawful structure, you know, geometry itself, exists independently of the matter it governs.

03:06.180 --> 03:07.960
So space-time is still a background fabric.

03:08.220 --> 03:12.500
Right. It might bend and it might warp, but it is still fundamentally a global container.

03:12.880 --> 03:13.260
Got it.

03:13.260 --> 03:20.040
And then, when modern cosmology layered thermodynamics on top of that, things got even more constrained.

03:20.780 --> 03:23.900
Thermodynamics, specifically the second law, gave us entropy.

03:24.160 --> 03:29.620
Right. And entropy gave us the arrow of time, the idea that things just naturally move from order to disorder.

03:29.800 --> 03:32.160
Exactly. A coffee cup shatters, right?

03:32.500 --> 03:34.960
It doesn't spontaneously put itself back together.

03:35.060 --> 03:36.880
I mean, that would be terrifying if it did.

03:36.880 --> 03:50.200
It would break all of physics. So the contemporary consensus is basically this expanding geometric universe where the forward march of time is entirely dictated by this statistical drift toward maximum messiness.

03:50.460 --> 03:51.380
Maximum messiness.

03:51.580 --> 03:51.700
Yeah.

03:52.180 --> 03:59.380
But Flyxion argues this consensus is fundamentally broken when you look at how the universe actually behaves on a macro scale, right?

03:59.380 --> 04:00.860
Yes. Completely broken.

04:00.860 --> 04:09.440
And to explore why it's broken, the paper focuses heavily on the work of physicist Julian Barber, specifically his 2020 framework from the book The Janus Point.

04:09.640 --> 04:12.020
Which is a brilliant piece of work in its own right.

04:12.060 --> 04:20.080
It really is. Barber looked at this whole inheritance, you know, absolute space, absolute scale, thermodynamic time, and basically said, we need to strip it all away.

04:20.360 --> 04:21.480
Every last bit of it.

04:21.540 --> 04:29.500
Right. He proposed that reality is nothing more than evolving relational configurations. He called it shape space.

04:29.500 --> 04:30.340
Shape space.

04:30.340 --> 04:30.700
Yes.

04:31.100 --> 04:37.780
Now, I really want to dig into the physical mechanics of this, because the paper highlights a very specific problem that Barber identified.

04:38.460 --> 04:40.060
The gravitational anomaly.

04:41.040 --> 04:43.000
Or, uh, the Boltzmann box.

04:43.420 --> 04:45.040
This is a crucial starting point.

04:45.440 --> 04:48.000
Let's think about standard thermodynamics for a second.

04:48.420 --> 04:51.320
You have a sealed box, and you pump gas into one corner.

04:51.560 --> 04:53.680
Okay, picture a glass box with a little puff of smoke.

04:53.680 --> 04:58.480
Right. When you let it go, the gas spreads out evenly until it fills the entire box.

04:58.480 --> 05:00.140
It reaches thermal equilibrium.

05:01.240 --> 05:02.120
Maximum entropy.

05:02.300 --> 05:03.440
The messiness we were talking about.

05:03.500 --> 05:03.980
Exactly.

05:04.160 --> 05:07.560
This is the classic model we use to understand the arrow of time.

05:07.560 --> 05:14.840
But Barber recognized a massive, totally glaring contradiction when you try to scale that box up to the size of the universe.

05:15.000 --> 05:18.120
Because the universe isn't a box of gas governed by random collisions.

05:18.120 --> 05:19.220
Oh, it's not.

05:19.260 --> 05:20.400
It is governed by gravity.

05:20.480 --> 05:21.080
Exactly.

05:21.560 --> 05:24.540
Gravity is the dominant force on the cosmological scale.

05:25.080 --> 05:28.460
And gravity does the exact opposite of spreading things out.

05:28.680 --> 05:29.660
Right. It pulls things in.

05:29.660 --> 05:36.680
If you have an unconfined system of particles governed by gravity, they don't diffuse into an even fog.

05:37.200 --> 05:38.080
They collapse.

05:38.480 --> 05:39.220
They clump together.

05:39.420 --> 05:39.520
Yeah.

05:39.840 --> 05:43.900
They form incredibly dense, complex, highly organized structures.

05:44.500 --> 05:48.420
Stars, accretion disks, solar systems, galaxies.

05:48.860 --> 05:53.480
So wait, I was trying to visualize this while reading, and I want to see if this analogy works for the underlying physics.

05:53.580 --> 05:54.280
Sure, let's hear it.

05:54.280 --> 05:59.300
So standard entropy is like dropping a normal deck of playing cards onto the floor.

05:59.640 --> 06:03.140
They scatter, they make a mess, you completely lose all the order.

06:03.200 --> 06:04.640
Right. Maximum mess.

06:04.640 --> 06:09.840
But gravitational evolution in Barber's framework is like dropping a deck of magnetic cards.

06:10.340 --> 06:16.180
You drop them, and instead of scattering, they snap together into these intricate, clumped, three-dimensional shapes.

06:16.460 --> 06:18.660
That is actually a fantastic way to visualize it.

06:18.720 --> 06:20.500
Okay, good. But here's where I get stuck.

06:20.600 --> 06:20.860
Hey.

06:20.860 --> 06:26.240
I get that they clump, but why does Barber claim this creates a new kind of time?

06:26.360 --> 06:27.820
Like, why does he call it N-taxi?

06:28.000 --> 06:33.000
That is the perfect question to ask, because N-taxi is not just a fancy synonym for clumping.

06:33.540 --> 06:35.400
Let's look at your magnetic cards again.

06:35.760 --> 06:40.960
When they snap together, they don't just form one solid, boring, undifferentiated block.

06:41.140 --> 06:46.920
They form complex, varied shapes with really specific internal relationships.

06:46.920 --> 06:53.040
Oh, like some cards might form a really tight core, while others stick out at odd angles?

06:53.200 --> 06:53.740
Exactly.

06:54.420 --> 07:01.680
Creating orbiting satellites and complex structures, Barber looked at the mathematics of the Newtonian N-body problem.

07:01.760 --> 07:04.200
Which is notoriously difficult math.

07:04.260 --> 07:05.800
It's basically impossible.

07:05.800 --> 07:10.660
It's the math used to calculate how multiple bodies interact gravitationally.

07:10.660 --> 07:18.780
It gets chaotic almost immediately, because you have to track the position and momentum of every single particle relative to every other particle all at the same time.

07:19.020 --> 07:19.640
A total nightmare.

07:19.860 --> 07:26.400
But Barber found a way to strip out the absolute space, the absolute time, and even the overall size of the system.

07:26.500 --> 07:27.700
Just completely ignored them.

07:27.700 --> 07:34.200
He factored them out mathematically, leaving only the pure relative shapes, the angles and ratios between the particles.

07:34.720 --> 07:35.880
This is his shape space.

07:36.040 --> 07:38.020
Okay, so it's just the geometry of the relationships.

07:38.580 --> 07:38.900
Yes.

07:39.520 --> 07:48.280
And when he ran the math on that, he discovered that as gravity pulls the system together, the variety of these shapes actually increases.

07:48.520 --> 07:49.000
Oh, interesting.

07:49.200 --> 07:52.800
So the system doesn't just get denser, it gets more structurally diverse.

07:52.800 --> 07:54.040
Precisely.

07:54.580 --> 07:57.820
And taxi is the mathematical measure of that structural differentiation.

07:58.420 --> 08:07.880
So Barber argued that the universe's arrow of time isn't driven by things getting messier, like entropy, but by things getting more structurally organized and complex.

08:08.000 --> 08:08.580
Which is intact.

08:08.780 --> 08:10.140
Okay, that totally makes sense.

08:10.520 --> 08:12.280
The complexity itself is the clock.

08:12.400 --> 08:12.780
Yes.

08:13.040 --> 08:18.580
Which naturally leads us to Barber's most famous and maybe most controversial concept, the Janus Point.

08:18.580 --> 08:23.700
Because if time is just the growth of complexity, what happens if you run the math backward?

08:24.420 --> 08:28.540
Well, if you run the n-body equations backward, gravity is essentially pulling in reverse.

08:28.700 --> 08:29.960
The clumps spread out.

08:30.000 --> 08:31.420
The magnetic cards unsnap.

08:31.480 --> 08:31.700
Right.

08:31.820 --> 08:32.880
The complexity drops.

08:33.180 --> 08:34.260
The intactity decreases.

08:34.600 --> 08:37.660
But, and this is key, it doesn't decrease infinitely.

08:37.840 --> 08:38.440
There's a floor.

08:38.820 --> 08:39.180
Yes.

08:40.140 --> 08:42.600
Barber found that there is a strict mathematical limit.

08:42.600 --> 08:49.660
There is a specific singular configuration where the particles are as evenly distributed as they can possibly be.

08:49.880 --> 08:52.340
A moment of minimum complexity.

08:53.060 --> 08:54.180
Maximum uniformity.

08:54.260 --> 08:56.440
And that singular moment is the Janus Point.

08:56.700 --> 09:00.380
Named after the Roman god with two faces looking in opposite directions.

09:00.540 --> 09:00.760
Right.

09:00.760 --> 09:09.260
Because from this singular point of maximum uniformity, the math dictates that the system must evolve outward in two temporal directions.

09:09.580 --> 09:12.960
The complexity grows in both directions away from that central pivot.

09:13.080 --> 09:14.540
So you get a bi-universe.

09:14.860 --> 09:15.040
Yeah.

09:15.160 --> 09:17.760
Two arrows at a time pointing away from each other.

09:17.800 --> 09:18.240
Exactly.

09:18.360 --> 09:23.460
It's mathematically beautiful, honestly, because it completely eliminates the need to ask, well, what happened before the Big Bang?

09:23.580 --> 09:24.900
It solves that problem neatly.

09:24.900 --> 09:30.320
Yeah, the Big Bang just becomes the central pinch point and time flows outward from it on both sides.

09:30.980 --> 09:35.860
But, um, here is where the Flyxion paper comes in like an absolute wrecking ball.

09:36.020 --> 09:36.880
It really does.

09:37.140 --> 09:43.540
Flyxion loves Barber's shift toward relational physics, obviously, but absolutely skewers the Janus Point.

09:44.200 --> 09:49.780
The paper argues that Barber accidentally smuggled a massive contradiction right into his own math.

09:50.100 --> 09:54.360
What's fascinating here is how subtle yet completely fatal the flaw is.

09:54.360 --> 09:57.100
Flyxion calls it the hidden global anchor.

09:57.420 --> 09:58.360
The hidden global anchor.

09:58.580 --> 10:03.080
Remember, Barber's entire goal was to get rid of absolute space and time.

10:03.320 --> 10:08.420
He wanted a purely relational universe where everything is just local geometry.

10:08.540 --> 10:08.680
Right.

10:08.760 --> 10:10.140
No giant clock on the wall.

10:10.380 --> 10:18.580
But to make the Janus Point work, you have to mathematically define a single universe-wide moment of minimum complexity.

10:18.840 --> 10:21.020
Oh, you have to coordinate the whole system at once.

10:21.340 --> 10:21.900
Precisely.

10:21.900 --> 10:31.260
To say this specific configuration is the origin point for everything, you are implicitly demanding that the universe possesses a globally privileged state.

10:31.460 --> 10:32.880
Right, you're looking at the whole box again.

10:32.960 --> 10:33.240
Yes.

10:33.760 --> 10:38.160
You are requiring all the particles everywhere to synchronize at a global minimum.

10:38.160 --> 10:45.720
Even if you stripped away Newton's clock, you've just replaced it with a hidden metatime, a global anchor that organizes the whole cosmos.

10:46.500 --> 10:52.040
But in a truly relational universe, there is no global container to do that coordinating.

10:52.540 --> 10:59.680
There is no top-down supervisory mechanism to tell the universe, okay, everyone, we've hit the Janus Point, start getting complex now.

10:59.680 --> 11:02.900
So Barber totally contradicted his own core premise.

11:02.980 --> 11:04.320
Flyxion's view, yes.

11:04.980 --> 11:12.740
So if Barber's bi-universe fails because it secretly relies on a global anchor, we are left with a massive problem.

11:12.860 --> 11:13.880
A huge gap.

11:14.200 --> 11:16.960
How do we explain why structure persists?

11:17.840 --> 11:26.380
Like how do things hold together and evolve if there is no global clock, no spatial box, and no universal origin point coordinating the show?

11:26.380 --> 11:33.760
And that transition is exactly what brings us to the absolute core of Flyxion's paper, the relativistic scalar vector plenum.

11:33.900 --> 11:34.880
RSVP for sure.

11:34.980 --> 11:35.500
RSVP.

11:35.600 --> 11:39.820
Flyxion throws out the spatial box, throws out the global clock, and throws out the Janus Point entirely.

11:40.040 --> 11:41.000
Just a clean sweep.

11:41.200 --> 11:45.460
In the RSVK framework, the fundamental physical metric of the universe isn't entropy.

11:46.060 --> 11:47.660
And it isn't Barber's and Taxi either.

11:47.800 --> 11:50.640
So the universe doesn't care about clumping for the sake of clumping?

11:50.640 --> 11:58.000
No. According to Flyxion, the only physically meaningful quantity in the universe is recursively maintainable coherence.

11:58.120 --> 12:00.120
Recursively maintainable coherence.

12:00.140 --> 12:00.520
Ah.

12:00.640 --> 12:02.640
Or what the paper simply calls admissibility.

12:03.420 --> 12:04.440
Yes, admissibility.

12:04.520 --> 12:08.900
We need to spend some serious time on this because it is basically the engine of the entire theory.

12:09.140 --> 12:09.260
Yep.

12:09.400 --> 12:15.100
So recursive means a process that feeds its output back into itself as an input. It's a loop.

12:15.300 --> 12:15.860
Exactly.

12:16.220 --> 12:20.320
And maintainable coherence means holding a structure together without it breaking down.

12:20.320 --> 12:23.640
So the core rule of reality in RSVP is this.

12:24.340 --> 12:27.260
Only recursively admissible configurations persist.

12:27.440 --> 12:28.360
That's the golden rule.

12:28.520 --> 12:30.640
Things don't exist because they are inside a box.

12:30.900 --> 12:37.680
They exist because their internal relationships continuously prove capable of surviving the transition to the next moment.

12:37.800 --> 12:38.040
Yes.

12:38.500 --> 12:43.880
In RSVP, the universe is not a single grand trajectory expanding outward from a cosmic origin.

12:44.200 --> 12:47.240
It is a decentralized, hyper-localized landscape.

12:47.240 --> 12:51.800
Structure emerges not from a global command, but from local negotiations.

12:52.060 --> 12:52.780
Local survival.

12:53.020 --> 12:53.200
Right.

12:53.400 --> 13:02.900
To survive, to be admissible, any given structure, whether it's a hydrogen atom, a star, or a biological cell, must constantly balance two opposing physical pressures.

13:03.060 --> 13:06.400
And Flyxion introduces these as Lamphron and Lamferdine.

13:06.560 --> 13:06.860
Yes.

13:07.760 --> 13:08.700
Lamphron and Lamferdine.

13:08.700 --> 13:08.900
Okay.

13:09.180 --> 13:10.060
Lamphron and Lamferdine.

13:10.180 --> 13:15.200
I read these terms and they honestly sound like proprietary pharmaceutical drugs.

13:15.520 --> 13:16.840
They do have that ring to them.

13:17.180 --> 13:19.320
Ask your doctor if Lamferdine is right for you.

13:19.400 --> 13:19.560
Yeah.

13:19.620 --> 13:21.420
But what do they actually mean physically?

13:21.640 --> 13:23.340
Like, what is the physical mechanism here?

13:23.520 --> 13:24.840
Let's break them down mechanically.

13:25.380 --> 13:27.480
Lamphron is the differentiation pressure.

13:27.480 --> 13:30.280
It is the drive toward local specification.

13:31.100 --> 13:31.120
Okay.

13:31.120 --> 13:37.880
Physically, it is the mechanism by which a system restricts its own degrees of freedom to form a concrete identity.

13:38.780 --> 13:43.620
When a cloud of gas collapses into a star, it is undergoing intense Lamphron pressure.

13:43.820 --> 13:45.720
Because it's getting highly specific.

13:45.820 --> 13:46.120
Yes.

13:46.300 --> 13:48.660
It is narrowing down the possible states it can occupy.

13:49.020 --> 13:51.880
It is creating highly specific localized constraints.

13:51.880 --> 13:56.880
So Lamphron is what makes a thing a specific thing, rather than just a vague cloud of potentials?

13:57.660 --> 13:58.220
Exactly.

13:58.720 --> 14:03.740
But when a system only experiences Lamphron, it becomes overly constrained.

14:03.960 --> 14:05.000
It isolates itself.

14:05.320 --> 14:07.880
And opposing this is Lamphrodine.

14:07.920 --> 14:08.420
The other pill.

14:08.680 --> 14:08.960
Right.

14:09.180 --> 14:12.080
This is the reintegration pressure, the smoothing dynamic.

14:12.840 --> 14:17.560
It forces a system to share operational information and energy with its environment.

14:17.960 --> 14:21.260
It forces the structure to communicate across scales.

14:21.880 --> 14:24.140
Okay, I'm tracking with you, but I really want to challenge this.

14:24.220 --> 14:24.740
Go for it.

14:24.840 --> 14:27.440
You're telling me Lamphrodine is a smoothing pressure.

14:28.220 --> 14:31.460
I'm sorry, but isn't that just classical entropy wearing a new hat?

14:31.780 --> 14:32.900
I can see why you think that.

14:33.040 --> 14:38.380
How is this reintegration pressure any different from standard thermodynamics trying to reach thermal equilibrium?

14:38.900 --> 14:42.880
It honestly sounds like you're just describing a hot cup of coffee cooling down to room temperature.

14:42.880 --> 14:46.280
That is exactly the pushback standard physicists would give.

14:46.380 --> 14:48.640
So let's unpack the mathematical distinction here.

14:48.740 --> 14:49.120
Please do.

14:49.120 --> 14:52.280
Classical entropy is derived from statistical mechanics.

14:52.840 --> 14:54.880
It's essentially about counting microstates.

14:55.580 --> 15:03.700
It assumes that if you leave a system alone, it will statistically drift toward the macrostate that has the highest number of possible random configurations.

15:04.160 --> 15:04.400
Right.

15:04.480 --> 15:05.340
It's just probability.

15:05.620 --> 15:05.880
Yes.

15:05.960 --> 15:08.420
It's a blind statistical inevitability.

15:08.920 --> 15:13.000
Lamphrodine, in the RSVP framework, is not a statistical drift.

15:13.320 --> 15:16.080
It is an active relational operator.

15:16.080 --> 15:17.080
An active operator.

15:17.240 --> 15:17.400
Yeah.

15:17.520 --> 15:17.960
Meaning what?

15:18.060 --> 15:18.320
Physically?

15:18.520 --> 15:22.500
Meaning it is the physical requirement for recursive continuity.

15:23.060 --> 15:24.100
Let's look at a star again.

15:24.740 --> 15:27.300
A star is extremely Lamphrodine heavy.

15:27.700 --> 15:30.560
It's a tight, highly differentiated constraint.

15:30.640 --> 15:30.760
Yeah.

15:30.820 --> 15:33.220
It's a huge ball of localized energy.

15:33.540 --> 15:37.560
But a star cannot survive purely on its own internal density.

15:37.560 --> 15:44.260
To remain recursively admissible to keep from collapsing into a black hole immediately, it must radiate energy outward.

15:44.400 --> 15:45.160
It has to shine.

15:45.320 --> 15:45.520
Right.

15:45.740 --> 15:47.800
It must couple with the broader environment.

15:48.480 --> 15:49.960
That radiation is Lamphrodine.

15:50.400 --> 15:56.040
It is actively redistributing relational information to maintain the local structural coherence of the star.

15:56.040 --> 15:56.560
Whoa.

15:56.960 --> 16:00.800
Entropy says the star radiates because the universe is statistically cold and empty.

16:01.560 --> 16:10.420
RSVP says the star radiates because if it didn't negotiate that reintegration, its internal constraints would become mathematically undefined and it would cease to persist.

16:10.640 --> 16:14.880
So Lamphrodine is literally a requirement for survival, not a statistical accident.

16:15.220 --> 16:15.720
Exactly.

16:15.740 --> 16:16.020
Okay.

16:16.200 --> 16:17.800
That is a profound difference.

16:17.900 --> 16:19.280
It's not passive decay.

16:19.760 --> 16:22.180
It's active structural negotiation.

16:22.540 --> 16:22.880
Yes.

16:22.880 --> 16:30.720
I want to test this with an analogy because this balance between Lamphrodine and Lamphrodine feels universally applicable.

16:30.960 --> 16:31.740
I'd love to hear it.

16:31.880 --> 16:33.380
Think of a growing startup company.

16:33.500 --> 16:33.720
Okay.

16:34.380 --> 16:38.460
In the very early days, you have like five people in a garage.

16:38.720 --> 16:38.880
Yeah.

16:39.040 --> 16:39.680
Everyone does everything.

16:39.840 --> 16:41.780
It's highly uniform, highly communicative.

16:42.260 --> 16:46.800
But to grow and persist, the company needs Lamphrodine pressure.

16:47.240 --> 16:49.500
It needs to specify and differentiate.

16:49.720 --> 16:49.880
Right.

16:49.940 --> 16:50.720
They need structure.

16:50.720 --> 16:59.120
So the CEO creates highly specialized departments, a dedicated marketing team, an independent software engineering team, a closed-door HR department.

16:59.700 --> 17:03.220
This Lamphrodine makes the company structurally complex and highly capable.

17:03.700 --> 17:08.080
They are narrowing their accessible future states by committing to specific operations.

17:08.280 --> 17:09.440
That maps perfectly.

17:09.800 --> 17:14.220
But if they push that Lamphrodine pressure too far, what happens physically to the structure?

17:14.220 --> 17:17.160
Well, the department's become completely isolated silos.

17:17.860 --> 17:19.260
Marketing doesn't talk to engineering.

17:19.640 --> 17:22.000
HR is entirely decoupled from product development.

17:22.360 --> 17:29.720
The structure becomes so over-constrained, so rigid in its specific identity, that it cannot adapt to a changing market.

17:30.820 --> 17:34.260
The company suffers what Flyxion calls terminal rigidity.

17:34.600 --> 17:35.640
And the structure shatters.

17:35.860 --> 17:36.160
Exactly.

17:36.280 --> 17:36.960
They go bankrupt.

17:36.960 --> 17:38.680
So how does the company save itself?

17:39.320 --> 17:42.340
The CEO has to introduce Lamphrodine pressure.

17:43.380 --> 17:45.180
They mandate open-floor communication.

17:45.480 --> 17:47.500
They force cross-departmental integration.

17:48.120 --> 17:51.420
They redistribute information to smooth out those rigid boundaries.

17:51.940 --> 17:56.860
They sacrifice some specific local identity for the sake of global coherence.

17:56.940 --> 17:58.180
Yes, the smoothing operator.

17:58.180 --> 18:01.180
But, and here's the failure mode on the other side.

18:01.460 --> 18:12.160
If the CEO pushes the Lamphrodine too far, if everyone is just endlessly communicating, having cross-functional meetings all day, and nobody has a specific differentiated job anymore.

18:12.200 --> 18:13.000
Nothing gets done.

18:13.240 --> 18:13.460
Right.

18:13.700 --> 18:15.480
The company suffers diffusive dissolution.

18:15.900 --> 18:19.660
It melts back into an unstructured mess, and again, it fails to persist.

18:19.780 --> 18:24.940
That analogy holds up structurally because it perfectly maps to Flyxion's definition of time.

18:25.100 --> 18:25.540
Wait, really?

18:26.020 --> 18:27.200
How did that map to time?

18:27.200 --> 18:30.880
In standard physics, time is the direction things get messy.

18:31.800 --> 18:34.660
In Barber's physics, time is the direction things get clumpy.

18:35.120 --> 18:38.180
But in RSVP, time is something entirely different.

18:38.740 --> 18:38.960
Okay.

18:39.200 --> 18:45.280
Time is simply the direction in which this Lamphrodine balance permits ongoing stabilization.

18:46.020 --> 18:48.440
Time is the direction of predictive continuity.

18:48.860 --> 18:51.260
The direction of predictive continuity.

18:51.620 --> 18:51.920
Wow.

18:52.120 --> 18:53.280
Let that sink in.

18:53.740 --> 18:55.660
Time isn't a river you are floating down.

18:55.660 --> 19:00.380
Time is just the set of moves you can make that don't result in your structure breaking apart.

19:00.540 --> 19:00.940
Exactly.

19:01.340 --> 19:04.460
Flyxion lays this out with a brilliant progression in the text.

19:04.720 --> 19:07.200
A crystal is highly ordered, right, but it's frozen.

19:07.620 --> 19:10.380
It has immense Lamphrodine, but zero Lamphrodine.

19:10.800 --> 19:15.420
A hurricane is locally coherent, but it dissipates quickly because it can't maintain its boundaries.

19:15.500 --> 19:16.340
Too much Lamphrodine.

19:16.340 --> 19:20.060
A star is dynamically structured, but it's a brute force burn.

19:20.060 --> 19:21.100
And a biosphere.

19:21.220 --> 19:23.060
A biosphere is recursively stabilized.

19:23.700 --> 19:24.540
Because it's alive.

19:24.720 --> 19:29.520
It is constantly, actively negotiating the boundary between differentiation and integration.

19:30.020 --> 19:33.440
A living organism is the ultimate manifestation of admissibility.

19:33.680 --> 19:34.740
Which is mind-blowing.

19:34.740 --> 19:37.300
But I have a massive mechanical question here.

19:37.580 --> 19:37.700
Sure.

19:38.100 --> 19:44.680
If there is no background space, no background time, and reality is just this local negotiation of admissibility.

19:45.200 --> 19:45.420
Yeah.

19:45.800 --> 19:47.340
How do we ever measure anything?

19:47.620 --> 19:48.700
That is the big hurdle.

19:48.880 --> 19:54.960
Like, how does a ruler or a clock or a telescope even exist physically to tell us what is going on?

19:55.140 --> 19:57.360
This is where we move into the mechanics of observation.

19:57.740 --> 20:00.160
And it's where the paper gets incredibly rigorous.

20:00.160 --> 20:07.820
To explain measurement without a background, Flyxion brings back a physical concept from Barber called Kepler pairs.

20:08.100 --> 20:08.660
Kepler pairs.

20:08.960 --> 20:18.240
When you have a chaotic cloud of particles interacting gravitationally, eventually two particles will fall into a stable, tightly bound orbit around each other.

20:18.360 --> 20:19.760
Like a binary star system.

20:19.880 --> 20:20.800
For example, yes.

20:21.260 --> 20:26.900
Barber noticed that these pairs act as isolated, highly regular subsystems within the chaos.

20:26.900 --> 20:30.340
Right, because their orbit is so stable, it essentially becomes a metronome.

20:30.560 --> 20:30.960
Exactly.

20:31.280 --> 20:32.820
But Flyxion takes us a step further.

20:33.440 --> 20:35.960
These Kepler pairs aren't just objects in the universe.

20:36.500 --> 20:40.740
They dynamically function as rods, compasses, and clocks for the universe.

20:40.880 --> 20:41.340
Oh, I see.

20:41.640 --> 20:43.620
They become the emergent reference baselines.

20:44.980 --> 20:49.160
Measurement isn't an abstract grid imposed on reality by a human scientist.

20:49.860 --> 20:56.400
Measurement is a physical, relational structure that has stabilized long enough to serve as an anchor for the structures around it.

20:56.400 --> 21:00.800
And this brings us to the math of how systems actually interact with these emergent clocks.

21:01.300 --> 21:05.540
The paper introduces an operator that fundamentally changes how we view reality.

21:05.620 --> 21:07.220
Yes, the projection operator.

21:07.360 --> 21:10.400
Represented by the mathematical symbol pi, the little hat on it.

21:10.900 --> 21:12.240
Now, let's walk through this carefully.

21:12.240 --> 21:22.860
In the RSVP framework, imagine the ultimate, massive, high-dimensional space of all the dynamically possible paths the universe could conceivably take.

21:23.120 --> 21:24.600
Every possible configuration.

21:24.680 --> 21:24.800
Right.

21:24.880 --> 21:26.500
This is called trajectory space, or X.

21:26.660 --> 21:32.560
But a local system, whether it's a galaxy or a human observer, cannot process or interact with all of X.

21:33.040 --> 21:37.200
The sheer amount of relational information would cause immediate diffusive dissolution.

21:37.380 --> 21:38.440
Your brain would just melt.

21:38.440 --> 21:39.280
Exactly.

21:39.980 --> 21:42.960
So, to survive, the system must compress reality.

21:43.300 --> 21:44.200
It must apply a filter.

21:44.560 --> 21:47.420
That filter is the projection operator, pi with a hat.

21:47.740 --> 21:50.300
So, how does that operator actually work mathematically?

21:50.640 --> 21:52.800
Like, what is it physically doing to the universe?

21:53.360 --> 21:54.620
Think of it like linear algebra.

21:55.480 --> 22:00.520
A projection matrix maps a high-dimensional vector space down into a lower-dimensional subspace.

22:01.000 --> 22:01.280
Okay.

22:01.500 --> 22:03.860
Flattening a 3D object into a 2D shadow.

22:04.000 --> 22:04.480
Precisely.

22:04.480 --> 22:12.800
When a physical system interacts with its environment, its internal constraints, its specific Lamphron identity act as this projection matrix.

22:13.180 --> 22:21.020
It forces the infinite possibilities of X to collapse into a much smaller, manageable geometry called an admissibility manifold.

22:21.480 --> 22:22.800
Denoted as M sub A.

22:23.100 --> 22:23.400
Yes.

22:23.820 --> 22:29.560
This manifold only contains the physical futures that are operationally compatible with the system's current structure.

22:29.560 --> 22:34.540
Here's where it gets really interesting, and honestly, a little terrifying.

22:34.660 --> 22:35.580
It is terrifying.

22:35.640 --> 22:37.540
Projection isn't just a passive lens.

22:37.680 --> 22:38.960
It is an active constraint.

22:39.400 --> 22:41.840
The paper calls this projection blindness.

22:42.180 --> 22:42.480
Yes.

22:42.820 --> 22:52.000
When you measure something, when you force the universe to interact with your specific Kepler pair or your specific telescope, you are forcing reality into a specific grammatical structure.

22:52.080 --> 22:52.260
Yeah.

22:52.260 --> 22:55.820
And by doing so, you actively restrict the futures that are available to you.

22:55.820 --> 23:00.640
This is perhaps Flyxion's most devastating critique of modern observational cosmology.

23:01.520 --> 23:03.400
Let's look at the expanding universe.

23:04.120 --> 23:11.900
Since Edwin Hubble, astronomers have looked at the red shift of distant galaxies, and they chose to project that data through a very specific mathematical grammar.

23:12.100 --> 23:13.460
The FLRW metric.

23:13.460 --> 23:18.620
Right. Which describes a universe where the fabric of space itself is metrically expanding.

23:18.880 --> 23:23.140
The classic balloon analogy. The galaxies are dots on a balloon, and the rubber is stretching.

23:23.320 --> 23:31.900
Yes. But Flyxion argues that this metric expansion isn't an absolute truth of the universe. It is merely an expansion-compatible namespace.

23:32.020 --> 23:32.340
Wow.

23:32.640 --> 23:36.020
It is a specific projection operator that we chose to use.

23:36.020 --> 23:41.640
And once we adopted that specific grammar, we became conceptually and operationally trapped.

23:42.000 --> 23:47.000
All our subsequent theories, all our new instruments, had to be compatible with that expanding namespace.

23:47.380 --> 23:55.400
I am starting to see the trap. So when we look deeper into space, and the data doesn't quite fit our balloon math, what do we do?

23:56.000 --> 23:57.260
We don't throw away the balloon.

23:57.440 --> 23:58.260
No, we don't.

23:58.280 --> 24:00.920
We invent invisible things to fix the math.

24:00.920 --> 24:07.220
Precisely. We observe galaxies spinning too fast for their visible mass, so we invent dark matter.

24:07.440 --> 24:10.600
We observe the redshift accelerating, so we invent dark energy.

24:11.180 --> 24:15.820
We have a uniformity problem in the early cosmic background, so we invent cosmic inflation.

24:16.240 --> 24:18.080
It's just patches on top of patches.

24:18.320 --> 24:25.120
In the RSVP framework, these mysteries, dark matter, dark energy, might not be physical entities at all.

24:25.120 --> 24:27.960
They might simply be stabilization artifacts.

24:28.460 --> 24:36.600
They are the mathematical ghosts we have to summon downstream to keep our original, arbitrary projection of an expanding metric coherent.

24:37.120 --> 24:45.200
We literally blinded ourselves by choosing a specific mathematical measuring stick, and now we are hallucinating 95% of the universe just to make the stick work.

24:45.300 --> 24:46.940
That is Fickshian's argument, yes.

24:47.080 --> 24:50.780
What happens to the actual physical reality that doesn't fit the projection?

24:50.780 --> 24:55.840
Mathematically, it falls into what is called the kernel of the projection, or the curve pi.

24:56.220 --> 24:58.960
In linear algebra, the kernel is the null space.

24:59.100 --> 25:02.060
It's everything that gets mapped to zero by the projection matrix.

25:02.140 --> 25:02.740
It's just deleted.

25:03.080 --> 25:09.900
In RSVP, when reality falls into the kernel of your measurement grammar, it becomes operationally unrecoverable.

25:10.300 --> 25:13.340
It is structurally deleted from your accessible future.

25:13.700 --> 25:18.460
It's a form of absolute physical blindness induced by the act of measurement itself.

25:18.460 --> 25:23.640
I really want to pause here, because this concept of projection blindness isn't just about astrophysics.

25:23.780 --> 25:25.340
No, it's deeply personal, too.

25:25.660 --> 25:37.320
If we are saying that adopting a specific coordinate system or a specific grammar literally deletes alternate futures and forces you to invent artifacts to stay sane,

25:37.580 --> 25:41.940
isn't that exactly how human memory and human worldviews work?

25:42.020 --> 25:43.060
It's a direct parallel.

25:43.060 --> 25:51.140
Like, if I adopt the rigid metric that financial wealth is the only measure of a successful life, I'm applying a massive projection operator to my reality.

25:51.540 --> 25:55.580
I collapse my infinite trajectory space down to a tiny admissibility manifold.

25:55.740 --> 25:57.120
You restrict your own universe.

25:57.300 --> 26:03.240
And any data that doesn't fit creative fulfillment, deep relationships, inner peace, falls into the kernel of my projection.

26:03.560 --> 26:05.980
It becomes operationally unrecoverable to me.

26:05.980 --> 26:13.560
That is an incredibly astute connection, and it is exactly the bridge Flyxion builds in the second half of the paper, in Section 7.

26:13.760 --> 26:15.020
Oh, this isn't just a metaphor.

26:15.220 --> 26:16.060
This is not a metaphor.

26:16.420 --> 26:22.500
Flyxion argues that the transition from cosmology to cognitive architecture is not a change of subject.

26:23.080 --> 26:28.740
The exact same mathematical operators, the projection matrix, the recursive admissibility,

26:29.220 --> 26:35.240
the balance of Lamphron and Lamphrodine that govern the survival of a galaxy, directly govern human cognition.

26:35.240 --> 26:37.760
A mind isn't a biological hard drive.

26:37.960 --> 26:41.220
It's a recursively stabilized admissibility manifold.

26:41.420 --> 26:41.900
Exactly.

26:42.220 --> 26:48.760
Let's dig into the physical mechanism of this in the brain, because usually we think of memory as a record of the past, a footprint in the mud.

26:49.080 --> 26:53.140
I experienced a thing, my brain took a snapshot, and stored it in a file cabinet.

26:53.320 --> 26:58.280
Yes, Barbara referred to these as fossilized geometric traces, passive records.

26:58.860 --> 27:00.880
But RSVP rejects this entirely.

27:01.460 --> 27:06.100
Flyxion introduces the concept of the admissibility log, or L sub et M.

27:06.160 --> 27:07.720
Okay, the admissibility log.

27:07.760 --> 27:11.700
In this framework, a memory is not retrospective, it is infrastructural.

27:12.240 --> 27:12.680
Infrastructural.

27:12.760 --> 27:15.080
Wait, explain how a memory operates as infrastructure.

27:15.340 --> 27:16.960
Think about neuroplasticity.

27:16.960 --> 27:24.840
When you learn a new skill, say, riding a bicycle, your brain isn't just recording a video of you riding a bike.

27:25.040 --> 27:25.260
Right.

27:25.480 --> 27:28.900
It is physically altering the synaptic weights between neurons.

27:29.480 --> 27:32.480
Neurons that fire together wire together, as Hebeam theory states.

27:32.880 --> 27:36.940
You are physically changing the transition matrix of your neural network.

27:37.180 --> 27:39.140
Okay, so the brain is physically different.

27:39.140 --> 27:42.820
That synaptic change is an active, operational constraint.

27:43.300 --> 27:49.140
It must be maintained right now in order to make the physical act of riding a bike tomorrow possible.

27:49.580 --> 27:52.420
So a record doesn't exist to tell me what happened in the past.

27:52.780 --> 27:56.340
A record is an active, physical constraint required to build the future.

27:56.540 --> 27:57.020
Exactly.

27:57.440 --> 28:02.160
If we connect this to the bigger picture, memory is the preservation of inferential continuity.

28:02.640 --> 28:07.980
If you lose a memory, if those synaptic constraints degrade, you are not just losing a piece of the past.

28:07.980 --> 28:12.260
Wait. You are literally amputating a branch of your possible futures.

28:12.400 --> 28:12.660
Yes.

28:13.020 --> 28:19.800
If I forget how to balance on a bike, the timeline where I cycle to work tomorrow is mathematically deleted from my admissibility manifold.

28:20.480 --> 28:21.900
It falls into the kernel.

28:22.280 --> 28:24.720
That is precisely how RSVP frames it.

28:25.080 --> 28:28.800
Time itself, in the brain, is just directional record utility.

28:29.520 --> 28:36.880
Time is simply the direction in which your past infrastructural constraints support, rather than block your future trajectories.

28:36.880 --> 28:37.760
That is incredible.

28:37.980 --> 28:40.820
Causes aren't billiard balls bumping into you from the past.

28:41.320 --> 28:47.400
Causes are the active, persistent admissibility constraints governing which futures you can physically access today.

28:47.940 --> 28:50.100
This completely rewrites cognitive health.

28:50.660 --> 28:56.580
If the brain is governed by these same cosmic pressures, then mental illness can be modeled as a failure of admissibility.

28:56.580 --> 28:58.680
The paper maps this out beautifully.

28:59.420 --> 29:06.580
If a neural network has excessive lamphron pressure, too much specialization, too much rigid constraint, it develops cognitive brittleness.

29:07.500 --> 29:08.400
Cognitive brittleness.

29:08.540 --> 29:11.620
The mind builds rigid, non-revisable models of the world.

29:12.140 --> 29:13.780
It cannot adapt to new data.

29:13.780 --> 29:18.620
You see this in deep trauma responses, rigid dogma, or obsessive compulsive loops.

29:19.040 --> 29:20.940
The structure is over-constrained.

29:21.200 --> 29:22.660
Terminal rigidity of the mind.

29:22.900 --> 29:26.820
And on the flip side, if the brain has excessive lamphrodine pressure.

29:27.040 --> 29:30.560
Too much reintegration, too much smoothing, without enough constraint.

29:30.560 --> 29:33.460
The result is cognitive dissolution.

29:34.300 --> 29:37.260
The neural representations become diffuse and incoherent.

29:37.640 --> 29:39.320
The boundaries of identity blur.

29:39.720 --> 29:44.640
This maps to states of psychosis, severe dissociation, or advanced cognitive decline.

29:45.420 --> 29:49.220
The mind loses the ability to maintain predictive continuity.

29:49.980 --> 29:55.240
A healthy mind, just like a stable galaxy, is an ongoing active achievement.

29:55.240 --> 30:01.420
It must constantly negotiate the balance between specifying its identity and remaining open to its environment.

30:01.820 --> 30:03.220
The symmetry is staggering.

30:03.520 --> 30:05.740
But I want to pull us back to the physics of the universe.

30:06.040 --> 30:11.300
Because if human cognition and cosmic evolution are both just local negotiations of admissibility,

30:11.680 --> 30:14.040
we run into a massive structural problem.

30:14.120 --> 30:14.460
Which is?

30:14.500 --> 30:16.880
How does the universe as a whole coordinate this?

30:17.000 --> 30:17.220
Ah.

30:17.760 --> 30:21.980
This brings us to a deep conflict outlined in the text between standard reconstruction

30:21.980 --> 30:25.040
and what Flyxion calls RSVP localism.

30:25.240 --> 30:29.940
To understand this conflict, we really need to look at how modern physics assumes structure is built.

30:30.420 --> 30:33.940
Standard physics relies on what we can call the aggregation-first model.

30:34.100 --> 30:34.880
Aggregation-first.

30:35.220 --> 30:40.380
Think about Fourier analysis, which is fundamental to quantum mechanics and signal processing.

30:40.700 --> 30:45.460
In standard Fourier analysis, to find the dominant frequencies of a signal,

30:45.800 --> 30:50.320
you have to collect the entire signal over time into a giant mathematical buffer,

30:50.640 --> 30:52.640
and then process the whole thing globally.

30:52.640 --> 30:57.080
Like trying to understand a jigsaw puzzle by assuming you have to dump all the pieces on the table

30:57.080 --> 30:58.940
and look at them from above to see how they fit.

30:59.320 --> 31:00.400
You need a global view.

31:00.540 --> 31:00.880
Yes.

31:01.140 --> 31:01.980
That's exactly it.

31:02.240 --> 31:04.920
The assumption is that coherence, the big picture,

31:05.180 --> 31:09.200
is something that is reconstructed after you have aggregated all the raw data.

31:09.660 --> 31:12.980
The geometry is primary, and the local interactions just fill it in.

31:13.120 --> 31:13.260
Right.

31:13.260 --> 31:15.280
But Flyxion flips this entirely.

31:16.060 --> 31:20.380
In RSVP localism, coherence exists prior to aggregation.

31:20.520 --> 31:22.200
Coherence prior to aggregation.

31:22.460 --> 31:23.900
How does that work mechanically?

31:24.040 --> 31:28.360
Like, how do the puzzle pieces know how to fit together if there is no global picture?

31:28.880 --> 31:34.080
Because structures emerge directly from local event-level admissibility relationships,

31:34.560 --> 31:37.120
the puzzle pieces don't need to know what the whole picture looks like.

31:37.160 --> 31:37.560
They don't.

31:37.560 --> 31:43.660
No, they only need to know if they can structurally bind to the piece immediately next to them without shattering.

31:43.980 --> 31:48.180
The universe behaves like a locality-preserving recursive filter.

31:48.440 --> 31:50.220
Okay, let me make sure I'm grasping this.

31:51.140 --> 31:57.040
You are saying there is no giant supervisory geometry coordinating the galaxy.

31:57.280 --> 31:57.880
None at all.

31:57.880 --> 32:05.580
A galaxy holds together simply because millions of local, neighbor-to-neighbor negotiations of Lamphron and Lampardine

32:05.580 --> 32:08.080
all successfully managed to not dissolve.

32:08.380 --> 32:08.680
Yes.

32:08.860 --> 32:12.380
The macro structure is just the survivor bias of microcoherence.

32:12.440 --> 32:14.580
That is a phenomenal way to summarize it.

32:14.960 --> 32:19.420
Large-scale order is just the macroscopic residue of locally persistent relationships

32:19.420 --> 32:21.740
that never lost their operational coherence.

32:21.880 --> 32:26.940
And this localism is exactly why Flyxion on violently rejects Barber's idea of the bi-universe.

32:26.940 --> 32:30.860
Right, the whole idea that time flows backward on the other side of the Janus point.

32:30.940 --> 32:35.240
Because the bi-universe concept implicitly requires a globally coherent trajectory.

32:35.500 --> 32:37.960
It requires a massive universe-wide mirror.

32:38.000 --> 32:39.560
Like that global anchor again.

32:39.840 --> 32:43.760
But if coherence is strictly local, there is no global mirror.

32:44.700 --> 32:49.120
Temporal direction emerges strictly locally from the preservation of predictive continuity.

32:49.580 --> 32:52.380
As Flyxion writes, and this line just gave me chills,

32:52.860 --> 32:55.340
time does not reverse, structures persist.

32:55.340 --> 32:56.600
It is so stark.

32:57.140 --> 32:57.800
It really is.

32:58.460 --> 33:03.400
A record, a structural constraint, is either operationally extensible into the next moment,

33:04.000 --> 33:04.780
or it breaks.

33:05.340 --> 33:08.420
You are either building a stable future, or you are dissolving.

33:08.600 --> 33:09.480
There is no backward.

33:10.040 --> 33:13.000
Time is just the direction that admissibility logs can be built.

33:13.120 --> 33:13.600
Exactly.

33:14.240 --> 33:17.260
There is no need for a cosmological mirror branch,

33:17.260 --> 33:22.120
because recursively admissible structures can only maintain predictive extension in one

33:22.120 --> 33:24.140
operational direction at a time.

33:24.360 --> 33:26.480
The local mechanism forbids a reversal.

33:26.480 --> 33:31.560
So if structure is entirely local, and space isn't actually stretching like a rubber band,

33:31.900 --> 33:33.680
and time is just the direction of survival,

33:34.180 --> 33:39.540
what on Earth happens when we zoom way, way out and look at the ultimate fate of the entire cosmos?

33:39.820 --> 33:41.620
It's pretty bleak in the standard model.

33:41.700 --> 33:45.300
Yeah, because the final sections of this paper completely rewrite the end of the world.

33:45.300 --> 33:50.220
The standard cosmological model tells us the universe will end in a heat death.

33:51.040 --> 33:57.060
Metric inflation will stretch space so fast that galaxies will recede from each other faster than light.

33:57.560 --> 33:58.540
Stars will burn out.

33:59.000 --> 34:00.440
Galaxies will drift apart.

34:00.560 --> 34:01.160
It'll be barking cold.

34:01.520 --> 34:05.260
And eventually, the supermassive black holes at the centers of these galaxies

34:05.260 --> 34:09.440
will slowly evaporate into nothingness through hawking radiation.

34:09.440 --> 34:15.340
The universe will be a dark, infinitely large, dilute soup of thermal chaos.

34:15.680 --> 34:16.580
Maximum entropy.

34:16.880 --> 34:19.960
But Flyxion offers a completely different interpretation of this expansion.

34:20.540 --> 34:22.380
The paper calls it void dominance.

34:23.080 --> 34:25.140
If space isn't a stretching rubber band,

34:25.480 --> 34:28.560
what is actually moving when we see galaxies Flyxion apart?

34:28.920 --> 34:34.240
In the RSVP framework, what we observe as metric expansion is actually topological redistribution.

34:34.500 --> 34:35.660
Topological redistribution.

34:35.660 --> 34:39.420
Remember, reality is just an active network of admissibility pathway.

34:39.440 --> 34:44.860
Over immense cosmic timescales, the rules of admissibility naturally favor smoothing

34:44.860 --> 34:47.300
lamphrodine pressure over terminal concentration.

34:47.740 --> 34:51.380
